mode: "100644"
text: "# Positioning, Point of View & Proof\n\nThis is what makes content *magnetic* instead of merely competent. A brand with a clear\nposition and a real point of view is easy to write distinctive content for; a brand without\none produces interchangeable posts no matter how good the writing is.\n\n## Positioning — three plain sentences\n\nGet these in the brand's own words, specific and jargon-free:\n\n1. **What it is:** \"[Business] is a [category] that helps [audience] [outcome].\"\n2. **What's different:** the one thing it does that the obvious alternatives don't. Force a\n   real answer — \"cheaper/better/faster\" is not a position. \"The only one that ___\" or\n   \"unlike [alternative], we ___\" is.\n3. **Why believe it:** the reason the difference is credible (see Proof below).\n\nIf the user can't name a difference, that's a finding, not a failure — capture \"parity\ncategory; differentiation is voice + consistency,\" which tells downstream skills to win on\npersonality and reliability rather than claims.\n\n## Point of view — the beliefs that make content shareable\n\nThe single most under-captured asset. A point of view is a **stance the brand will defend**\n— ideally one a reasonable person could disagree with. It's what turns a feed of updates\ninto a feed worth following.\n\nElicit it:\n\n- \"What does almost everyone in your space get wrong?\"\n- \"What do you believe that your competitors would never say out loud?\"\n- \"What advice do you give customers that they don't want to hear?\"\n- \"What are you *against*?\"\n\nCapture 1–3 POVs the brand will stake out, and — just as important — note the takes it\n*won't* make (off-limits, too risky, not authentic). Content skills use these to give posts a\nspine; `contrarian-and-opinion` owns the craft of publishing them well (the narrowness test,\nsteelmanning, and the ragebait line).\n\n> A brand that believes nothing in particular will sound like everyone. Find the conviction.\n\n## Proof — the credible specifics\n\nVague authority (\"trusted by industry leaders\") persuades no one. Collect what a skeptic\nwould actually accept:\n\n- **Numbers:** customers, revenue, results, percentages, time saved, growth.\n- **Named customers / logos** (only those cleared to use publicly).\n- **Outcomes / case results:** \"cut onboarding from 3 weeks to 2 days.\"\n- **Credentials / track record:** years, certifications, notable work, awards.\n- **Social proof:** reviews, ratings, community size, testimonials.\n\nNote any usage restrictions (which logos/figures are public, which need approval — and for\ntestimonials/UGC, **which permissions you actually hold**: organic reposting and paid-ad use\nare different rights). Downstream skills will reach for these constantly; the more concrete,\nthe better the content. Two routing rules: numbers get verified before publishing\n(`content-research-and-sourcing` — no stat ships unsourced), and testimonials/reviews follow\n`social-proof-and-testimonials`' rules (FTC: real, typical, disclosed where required — never\nfabricated or cherry-picked past honesty).\n\n## Offers & CTAs — what we actually want people to do\n\nCapture the real calls to action so content drives something:\n\n- Primary CTA (sign up, book a demo, shop, subscribe, visit, donate).\n- Secondary/soft CTAs (follow, comment, save, join the community, read more).\n- Lead magnets / free tools / offers worth pointing to.\n- Any \"we never hard-sell on [platform]\" rules.\n\n## Competitor contrast (optional but powerful)\n\nIf relevant, capture how the brand wants to *sound and stand* relative to named competitors —\nnot to attack them, but to sharpen its own edges. \"They're corporate and safe; we're direct\nand a little irreverent.\" This contrast helps every content skill find the open lane.\n"
